WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our first guests tonight are
two talented actors you know

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.004
for their work
in movies like "Aquaman"

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.339
and "The Guardians
of the Galaxy."

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.474
They both star
in season two of "See,"

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.643
which is streaming now
on Apple TV+

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:12.312
with new episodes every Friday.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.579
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:17.917
-Everyone close to you
always ends up suffering.

00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:24.090
Now it's your turn.

00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:30.296
-Leave this place, Edo.

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.865
Please.

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.833
It is over.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:37.036
-No, it's not over.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:38.938
Not while you're
still breathing.

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:43.109
-Please welcome to the show
Jason Mamo-- Momoa

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.744
and Dave Bautista. Gentlemen.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.713
Ma-Ma-Ma-Ma-Ma!
[ Laughter ]

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:49.115
How are you, fellas?
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.117
-Better watch your ass,
Seth Meyers.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:54.020
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, you're safely in London.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.224
I have nothing to worry about.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.959
Thank you for staying up late,
Jason.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:01.427
I know you're in London,

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:04.564
and I do appreciate you making
the time for us this evening.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:08.100
I also heard -- not to make
things boring for people

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.135
who don't know what
we're talking about,

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.771
that you're also a West Ham fan.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:15.675
-I am. Yes, we are. We do.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.310
I didn't know
you're a West Ham fan.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.945
-I am.
I have been for like 20 years.

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.746
How did that happen?
Oh, my God, look at that.

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:23.382
You got an actual tattoo?

00:01:23.382 --> 00:01:26.118
How did you become
a West Ham fan?

00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:28.822
-Oh, just 'cause we're family
and that's kind of what you --

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.424
the first place that you live
when you kind of come to London.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.692
You got to claim it, so that's
what it ended up being.

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:34.994
-Well done. This is --

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.428
-I didn't know you were.

00:01:36.428 --> 00:01:38.998
-I was, like, doing --
I was in London 20 years ago,

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:41.367
and I decided that I was
going to pick one team

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.337
and go see a game and it was
West Ham and there you go.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:45.938
-There you go.

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:47.806
-One day we'll go
to a game together.

00:01:47.806 --> 00:01:50.576
-Our Hammers.
-So this is very exciting.

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:52.411
This is the second season
of the show.

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.247
Dave, you joined the cast.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.315
You're playing Jason's
younger brother

00:01:56.315 --> 00:02:00.185
in a future where people
have lost the ability to see.

00:02:00.185 --> 00:02:01.654
How did you get involved?

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.657
Did you know about the show
before you came onboard?

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.026
-Yeah, I actually --
I was a fan of the show

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:11.297
before I even was contacted
about playing Jay's brother.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.366
The funny thing, you know, that
sticks out when you say that

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:16.936
is I'm like 10 years,
maybe more, older than Jay.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.238
The first thing they said was,

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:19.872
"We want you to play
his younger brother."

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:21.640
And I was like,
"How's that going to work?"

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.342
[ Laughs ]

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:25.811
So they aged Jay up a lot
and tried to young me down.

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:28.247
I don't know if it worked,
but, you know, maybe we're --

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:30.950
I'm a year younger, but --
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.919
Obviously older.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.054
But, yeah, I wanted to work with
Jay for years.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:38.357
I actually had reached out to
him like maybe a year and a half

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:40.259
before on Instagram.

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:44.230
I DM'd -- I slipped into his --
creeped into his DMs to say --

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.398
anyway --
just 'cause I was a fan,

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:46.665
I wanted to work with the dude
so bad.

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:48.400
So, anyway, yeah,
he contacted me

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.302
about the part and I signed up.

00:02:50.302 --> 00:02:54.306
-Where did you guys first meet?

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:55.774
-Comic-Con.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:57.109
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:59.245
-Yeah. We both geeked out
on each other at Comic-Con.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.712
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:03:00.712 --> 00:03:02.114
-Were other people
there to see it?

00:03:02.114 --> 00:03:05.217
Because I imagine if you were
just, you know, a civilian

00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:07.653
at Comic-Con and you watched
the two of you guys meet,

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:10.123
that would be well worth
the price of admission.

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:12.558
-Absolutely.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:13.626
-I don't know. I don't know.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:14.794
There wasn't
a whole lot of people.

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:17.496
It was kind of
a behind-the-curtain thing,

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:18.931
but there was a few people
around, for sure.

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:21.000
-You also worked together
on "Dune."

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.535
And I've seen stills
from "Dune."

00:03:23.535 --> 00:03:26.705
And it looks incredibly hot
and uncomfortable.

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:30.843
And then "See" looks incredibly
cold and uncomfortable.

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:34.079
Is this true or is this
just special effects?

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:38.383
-It's absolutely true.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.383 --> 00:03:42.521
"See" was in Toronto in
the winter, which is brutal.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:44.523
And we both pretty much had --

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:46.492
I mean, I know I barely
have any clothes on.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:49.328
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:50.596
-But I like the cold.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:52.631
Dave I think --
Dave, you like the heat, right?

00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:55.300
-I like the heat, yeah.
Yeah, we're opposite.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.070
I didn't suffer
on "Dune" at all.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.605
I kind of loved it.

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:02.007
But on "See," I had
layers and layers and layers,

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:03.743
and I was still freezing.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:07.313
But I don't have a full head of
hair to keep my head warm.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:10.082
I had to have blankets over
my head trying to keep me warm.

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:11.517
But I just don't like the cold.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.285
Don't like it at all.
Me and the cold don't get along.

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:16.122
-I'm very surprised to hear that
you like the cold, Jason.

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:18.223
You struck me as also
a warm-weather guy.

00:04:18.223 --> 00:04:23.129
And I believe that you guys both
have history as lifeguards.

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.431
-I mean, I was raised
in Iowa, Seth.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.933
So, I mean, I had, like,
really horrible winters

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:31.337
and really horrible summers
in the Midwest, so --

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:32.938
But I don't know.
I just like the cold.

00:04:32.938 --> 00:04:35.675
But I didn't know, Bautista,
you were a lifeguard?

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:37.176
-I just found out yesterday
that you were.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.611
Yeah, I was a lifeguard.

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:42.314
I lifeguarded from the time
I was like 16 till I was 24.

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:44.750
It was just, like,
the easiest summer gig ever.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.319
And I would just refuse
to give it up.

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.687
So I was like the guy
who's still lifeguarding

00:04:48.687 --> 00:04:50.322
who was way too old
to be lifeguarding.

00:04:50.322 --> 00:04:52.692
[ Laughs ]

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:56.095
-I get it -- my dad, I mean,
he put me in junior lifeguards,

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:57.296
and so we kind of learned
how to --

00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:58.731
that way, you just learn
how to be in the ocean.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:03.369
And so all my family
were true lifeguards.

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:04.871
My great uncle is
Buffalo Keaulana.

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:06.405
He saved many, many, many lives.

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:09.575
So it was kind of an honor
where you come from in Hawaii.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.144
I wasn't sitting by a pool.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.045
Not that there's anything wrong
with that.

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:15.080
-I was sitting by the pool, man.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.550
-It's a big-ass wave, Seth.
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.686
-Biggest lifeguards to the worst
neighborhood pools, man.

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:23.656
Just have all these bad kids
just running around.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.991
-In the 8 to 10 years
that you were a lifeguard, Dave,

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:28.860
did you have to do
any thrilling rescues?

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.596
-No. I mean, the water,
you know,

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.831
it only really went up
to like six feet.

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:34.400
It was like six feet deep.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.103
I was like, "Don't make me jump
in," and I was still --

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:39.538
But, no, never --
like, I never saved a life.

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:43.309
It never came to that,
but I was always just kind of

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.611
hanging around, yelling at kids.

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:48.580
-Yeah, I would feel like you
probably wouldn't run too fast

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.083
on a pool if Dave Bautista
was in the chair.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:52.952
-No.
-Yeah.

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.253
-The guy I used to work with,

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.789
he was a childhood buddy
of mine, too.

00:05:56.789 --> 00:06:00.159
And he was a football player
who was just as big as I was.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:02.027
They, literally -- They'd send
us out to, like,

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:04.229
the worst neighborhoods
to lifeguard the pools

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:05.898
'cause the kids were
just rowdy as hell.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:08.500
We'd just yell at them all day.
We'd just yell at them.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:09.535
And that's typically
what it took.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:10.536
-There you go.

